# Illustrative log excerpt — reconstructed from the field values and query logic
# documented in this lab's README (Stage 1). Field names match real Windows
# Security Event Log / Splunk output for Event ID 4662; provided as an
# annotated teaching example, not a raw export.
#
# NOTE: this example intentionally shows the DOCUMENTED DETECTION GAP from
# the lab — the recon activity itself does NOT appear here, because native
# 4662 auditing did not capture it when run as a privileged account locally
# on the DC (see README "Detection Gap - Stage 1" and playbooks/01-ldap-recon-response.md
# for why PowerShell Script Block Logging or EDR telemetry is needed instead).

EventCode=4662
_time=2026-08-08T01:58:03
Account_Name=WIN-AUHSOB0S2PO$        # <-- DC's own machine account (routine housekeeping)
SubjectUserName=WIN-AUHSOB0S2PO$
ObjectName=CN=Policies,CN=System,DC=lab,DC=local
Accesses=Write Property

# --- What this demonstrates ---
# Every 4662 event observed during the actual LDAP recon test window came
# from the DC's own machine account doing routine internal writes — never
# from the account that ran the [adsisearcher] enumeration queries. This is
# the evidence behind the lab's documented finding: local, privileged LDAP
# reads on the DC console do not reliably trigger 4662, even with a fully
# and correctly configured audit pipeline (Audit Directory Service Access +
# SACL + Splunk Universal Forwarder, all validated working via this event).
